eratorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=2$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy-2x=0$ is {{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186152571 :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=2$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy-2x=0$ is {{field-3}}." "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=2$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy-2x=0$ is {{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186311186 :answer-field/correct 17592186310010 17592186310010] [:db/cas 17592186311372 :answer-field/correct 17592186309443 17592186309443] [:db/cas 17592186311543 :answer-field/correct 17592186309437 17592186309437] [:db/cas 17592186311442 :answer-field/correct 17592186309819 17592186309819] [:db/cas 17592186311479 :answer-field/correct 17592186310760 17592186310760] [:db/cas 17592186311483 :answer-field/correct 17592186309660 17592186309660] [:db/cas 17592186311430 :answer-field/correct 17592186309634 17592186309634] [:db/cas 17592186311489 :answer-field/correct 17592186309337 17592186309337] [:db/cas 17592186311516 :answer-field/correct 17592186308752 17592186308752] [:db/cas 17592186311324 :answer-field/correct 17592186310102 17592186310102] [:db/cas 17592186311453 :answer-field/correct 17592186309791 17592186309791] [:db/cas 17592186311507 :answer-field/correct 17592186308815 17592186308815] [:db/cas 17592186311007 :answer-field/correct 17592186308860 17592186308860] [:db/cas 17592186311373 :answer-field/correct 17592186310660 17592186310660] [:db/cas 17592186311469 :answer-field/correct 17592186309054 17592186309054] [:db/cas 17592186079163 :question/worked-solution nil "The operator has constant coefficients. Applying it to $y=e^{4x}$ gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y)&=y''-4y'\\\\&=16e^{4x}-4(4e^{4x})\\\\&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186077756 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186077759] [:db/cas 17592186079223 :question/worked-solution nil "For $y=e^x+x,$ the required derivatives are $y'=e^x+1,$ $y''=e^x,$ and $y'''=e^x.$ Hence\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y)&=y'''-2y''+2y'\\\\&=e^x-2e^x+2(e^x+1)\\\\&=e^x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078116 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186078120] [:db/cas 17592186265866 :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{3}=1,{a}_{2}=0,{a}_{1}=2,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\right){\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({\\sin }^{2}⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sin }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{3}=1,{a}_{2}=0,{a}_{1}=2,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({\\sin}^{2}x) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\right){\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({\\sin }^{2}⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sin }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078335 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}}+3 \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}+5,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=e^{- x}?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{2}}{\\textrm{d} x^{2}}+3 \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x}+5,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=e^{- x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186078335 :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = a_{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + a_{1}\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + a_0y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  $a_2 = 1, a_1 = 3,$ and $a_0 = 5.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+5\\right){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{-x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-x})+5{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{e}^{-x})+3\\cdot (-{e}^{-x})+5{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-3{e}^{-x}+5{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = a_{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{2}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{2}} + a_{1}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d} x} + a_0y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  $a_2 = 1, a_1 = 3,$ and $a_0 = 5.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+5\\right){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({e}^{-x})+3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-x})+5{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{e}^{-x})+3\\cdot (-{e}^{-x})+5{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-3{e}^{-x}+5{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186079164 :question/worked-solution nil "For $y=x^3,$ we have $y'=3x^2,$ $y''=6x,$ and $y'''=6.$ Applying the variable-coefficient operator gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y)&=x^2y'''-xy''+y'\\\\&=6x^2-x(6x)+3x^2\\\\&=3x^2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186077762 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186077767] [:db/cas 17592186078336 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=x^2\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}}{\\textrm d x^{3}}-5x \\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}} +9\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\ln{x}?$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=x^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{3}}{\\textrm{d} x^{3}}-5x \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{2}}{\\textrm{d} x^{2}} +9\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d} x},\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\ln{x}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186078336 :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = x^2, f_2 = -5x, f_1 = 9,$ and $f_0 = 0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\left({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-5x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+9\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-5x\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{3}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{2}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d} x} + f_0(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = x^2, f_2 = -5x, f_1 = 9,$ and $f_0 = 0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\left({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-5x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)+9\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)-5x\\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\right)+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186078215 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle D(y)=y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} - \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} + y^3,\n$$ then calculate $D(e^{3x}).$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle D(y)=y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{3}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{3}} - \\dfrac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + y^3,\n$$ then calculate $D(e^{3x}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186078215 :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} +  f_{2}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = y,  f_2 = 0,  f_1 = -1,$ and $f_0 = y^2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y = e^{3x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({e}^{x}) & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({e}^{3x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x})+{({e}^{3x})}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(3{e}^{3x})-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{3x})-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & =27{e}^{6x}-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}({e}^{6x}+27{e}^{3x}-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{3}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{3}} +  f_{2}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{2}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{2}} + f_{1}(x,y)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d} x} + f_0(x,y)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = y,  f_2 = 0,  f_1 = -1,$ and $f_0 = y^2.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y = e^{3x}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D({e}^{x}) & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({e}^{3x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x})+{({e}^{3x})}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(3{e}^{3x})-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{3x})-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & =27{e}^{6x}-3\\cdot {e}^{3x}+{e}^{9x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}({e}^{6x}+27{e}^{3x}-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124899 :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent and ${v}_{4}=3{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+5{v}_{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=3{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+5{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186124899 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}$” is true. Indeed, solving equation ${v}_{4}=3{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+5{v}_{3}$ for ${v}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{v}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{v}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has only zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0$” is false. Since ${v}_{4}=3{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}+5{v}_{3},$ we have that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot {v}_{1}-2\\cdot {v}_{2}+5\\cdot {v}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {v}_{4}=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement “${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set” is false. Since ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution, the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set by definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}$” only." "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3},$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$” is true. Indeed, solving equation ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=3{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+5{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ for ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+\\frac{5}{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3},$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}$ has only zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}$” is false. Since ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=3{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+5{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},$ we have that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-2\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+5\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly independent set” is false. Since ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution, the set $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly dependent set by definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3},$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186449320 :answer-field/key "${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}$" "${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},$ ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3},$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$"] [:db/cas 175921864