is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot (2{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}0+{x}_{2}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}(2{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${0,{v}_{1},2{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent” is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}+0\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent”; “${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent” only." "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})+{x}_{2}({\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3})+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{3}){\\mathbf{v}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0},$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{\\mathbf{0},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot \\mathbf{0}+\\mathbf{0}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+\\mathbf{0}\\cdot (2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}\\mathbf{0}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{3}(2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{\\mathbf{0},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+b\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+b\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+\\mathbf{0}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent”; “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent” only."] [:db/cas 17592186449323 :answer-field/key "${{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" "$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent"] [:db/cas 17592186449324 :answer-field/key "${0,{v}_{1},2{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent" "$\\{\\mathbf{0},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent"] [:db/cas 17592186449325 :answer-field/key "${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent" "$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent"] [:db/cas 17592186244007 :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly dependent and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is linearly dependent and $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186244007 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution” is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$” is true. Indeed, solving equation ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ for ${v}_{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{4}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{3}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, ${x}_{4}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution, which contradicts the fact that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\nHence, ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}.$\n\n- Statement “${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$” is false. Since the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent set, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nhas no non-zero solution. Hence, ${v}_{3}$ is not a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution”; “${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$” only." "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution” is true. Since $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement “${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$” is true. Indeed, solving equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}$ for ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{4}}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{4}}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, ${x}_{4}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution, which contradicts the fact that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\nHence, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}.$\n\n- Statement “${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$” is false. Since the set $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent set, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas no non-zero solution. Hence, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ is not a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution”; “${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186448944 :answer-field/key "${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution" "${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution"] [:db/cas 17592186448945 :answer-field/key "${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$" "${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$"] [:db/cas 17592186448946 :answer-field/key "${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$" "${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$"] [:db/cas 17592186244008 :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186244008 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “${{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent” is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}(2{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “${{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent” is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{2}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {v}_{1}+1\\cdot {v}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent”; “${{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent” only." "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3})+{x}_{2}({\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3})+{x}_{3}(2{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}) & =\\mathbf{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}){\\mathbf{v}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0},$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+b\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0},$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+a\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+b\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+1\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot ({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent”; “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” only."] [:db/cas 17592186448947 :answer-field/key "${{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3}