{v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent”; “${{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent” only." "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})+{x}_{2}({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3})+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0},$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})+1\\cdot ({\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{1})=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})+{x}_{2}({\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{1})=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{