$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}0=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” only." "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})+1\\cdot ({\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3})+(-1)\\cdot ({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3})=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})+{x}_{2}({\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3})+{x}_{3}({\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3})=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},3{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}(2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2})+{x}_{3}(3{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}) & =\\mathbf{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+2{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+3{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0},$ which means that \n\n$$\n\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},3{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},\\mathbf{0}\\}$ is linearly independent” is false. Indeed, notice that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+\\mathbf{0}\\cdot {\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+1\\cdot \\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\mathbf{0}=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},\\mathbf{0}\\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},3{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent” only."] [:db/cas 17592186454352 :answer-field/key "$\\{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{1}-{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent" "$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent"] [:db/cas 17592186454353 :answer-field/key "$\\{{v}_{1},2{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent" "$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},2{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},3{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent"] [:db/cas 17592186454354 :answer-field/key "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},0\\}$ is linearly independent" "$\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},\\mathbf{0}\\}$ is linearly independent"] [:db/cas 17592186407778 :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},\\text{ }{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent one, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly independent set and $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},\\text{ }{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly dependent one, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186407778 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$, ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$” is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution. Moreover, ${x}_{1}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent.\nTherefore, from ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{1}}{v}_{3}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{1}}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$, ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement “${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$” is false. Since ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{2}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{2}+{v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}$” is false. Since ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set, ${v}_{3}$ can not be a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}.$ As a result ${v}_{2}+{v}_{3}$ can't be written as combination of ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{4}$ too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{2}$, ${v}_{3}$ and ${v}_{4}$” only." "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$” is true. Since $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution. Moreover, ${x}_{1}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution and that's a contradiction as $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is linearly independent.\nTherefore, from ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}+{x}_{4}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}=\\mathbf{0},$ we get\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}=-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{1}}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}-\\frac{{x}_{4}}{{x}_{1}}{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3},$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement “${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$” is false. Since $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ can not be a linear combination of other vectors.\n\n- Statement “${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$” is false. Since $\\{{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},{\\mathbf{v}}_{3},{\\mathbf{v}}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly independent set, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ can not be a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}.$ As a result ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ can't be written as combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$ too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ is a linear combination of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$, ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{4}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186454833 :answer-field/key "${v