st calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-3}{-3}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=3\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-3}{-3}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=3\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=3\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186284729 :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=-2+6\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." "Write the complex number $z=-2+6\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta$ in radians to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186339034 :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{e}}^{11\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339035 :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the third quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186339036 :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the second quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)| \\\\  & =\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get \n\n$$\nz=4\\,{\\text{e}}^{2\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348443 :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=1+2\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." "Write the complex number $z=1+2\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta$ in radians to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186348446 :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=5-\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." "Write the complex number $z=5-\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta$ in radians to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186348446 :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{5}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)| \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.197\\,39… \\\\  & ≈6.086.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{26}$ and $\\theta =6.086$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{5}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)| \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.197\\,39… \\\\  & ≈6.086.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{26}$ and $\\theta =6.086$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186348453 :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\,{\\text{e}}^{5\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186371541 :question/problem "Write the complex number $z=3-2\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." "Write the complex number $z=3-2\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta$ in radians to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186373250 :question/worked-solution "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-2}{2}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-2}{2}\\right)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=2\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{e}}^{7\\pi \\text{i}/4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088920 :tutorial/content "**Euler's formula** allows us to convert between complex exponential functions and complex numbers expressed in polar form. It states that\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWith Euler's formula, we can write any complex number $z$ in three equivalent ways. Letting $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\textrm{arg}(z),$ we have the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">****</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Cartesian</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Polar</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Exponential</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+\\textrm{i}y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r\\left( \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFor instance, if $z=1+\\textrm{i},$ then we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\theta  & =\\text{arg}(z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the complex number $z=1+\\textrm{i}$ can be written in exponential form as\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi/4}.\n$$\n\nWhen $\\theta=\\pi,$ we have a special case:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0\n$$\n\nThe equation $e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0$ is called **Euler's identity**. It is often described as the most beautiful equation in all of mathematics because it combines five important constants $e, \\textrm{i}, \\pi, 1,$ and $0$ into a single elegant form." "**Euler's formula** allows us to convert between complex exponential functions and complex numbers expressed in polar form. It states that\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWith Euler's formula, we can write any complex number $z$ in three equivalent ways. Letting $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\textrm{arg}(z),$ we have the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Cartesian</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Polar</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Exponential</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$z$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$x+\\textrm{i}y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$r\\left( \\cos\\theta + \\textrm{i}\\sin\\theta\\right)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFor instance, if $z=1+\\textrm{i},$ then we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\theta  & =\\text{arg}(z)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{1}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the complex number $z=1+\\textrm{i}$ can be written in exponential form as\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{2}e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi/4}.\n$$\n\nWhen $\\theta=\\pi,$ we have a special case:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} = -1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0\n$$\n\nThe equation $e^{\\textrm{i}\\pi} +1=0$ is called **Euler's identity**. It is often described as the most beautiful equation in all of mathematics because it combines five important constants $e, \\textrm{i}, \\pi, 1,$ and $0$ into a single elegant form."] [:db/cas 17592186120320 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}=-4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=-4{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}-4{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." "We say that two vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}=-4\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=-4{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-4{\\mathbf{a}}_{1} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 4{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186120321 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vecto