he set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent” only." "We say that two vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$ is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$” is false. Since statement $-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$ is true, we have ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}$ for ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$\n\n- Statement “The set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent” is true. Since statement $-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$ is true, the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$; “The set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent” only."] [:db/cas 17592186454462 :answer-field/key "$-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$" "$-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186454463 :answer-field/key "${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$" "${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186454464 :answer-field/key "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent." "The set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186511507 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+2{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent."] [:db/cas 17592186511507 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12+0+2 \\\\ 3+6+4 \\\\ 0+0+10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "We say that three vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+2{\\mathbf{a}}_{3} & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-12+0+2 \\\\ 3+6+4 \\\\ 0+0+10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne \\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186564502 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186564502 :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-2{a}_{2} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "We say that two vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne \\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186572667 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+2{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186572667 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}+2{a}_{3} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6-4-2 \\\\ 9+5-14 \\\\ -3+3+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=3,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "We say that three vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+2{\\mathbf{a}}_{3} & =3\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -14 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6-4-2 \\\\ 9+5-14 \\\\ -3+3+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ for ${x}_{1}=3,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186119498 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]},\\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]},\\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent."] [:db/cas 17592186119498 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne \\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 7{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 7{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186120300 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186120300 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements “The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$” and “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent” are false, while statement “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” is true." "We say that three vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo so