3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(\\mathbf{0})=\\mathbf{0}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(\\mathbf{0})+2(\\mathbf{0})=\\mathbf{0}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$, we get that the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements “The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” and “The equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions” are false, while statement “The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly independent” is true."] [:db/cas 17592186468801 :answer-field/key "The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly independent."] [:db/cas 17592186468802 :answer-field/key "The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186468803 :answer-field/key "The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions." "The equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186433173 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186433173 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent” is false, while statements “The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” and “The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions” are true." "We say that three vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{2}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-5 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement “The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly independent” is false, while statements “The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly dependent” and “The equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions” are true."] [:db/cas 17592186475328 :answer-field/key "The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly dependent."] [:db/cas 17592186475329 :answer-field/key "The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions." "The equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$  has an infinite number of nonzero solutions."] [:db/cas 17592186475330 :answer-field/key "The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." "The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly independent."] [:db/cas 17592186564487 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solution." "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nII. The vectors ${\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{a}}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solution."] [:db/cas 17592186564487 :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-13{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)+9(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true." "We say that three vectors ${\\mathbf{v}}_{1},{\\mathbf{v}}_{2},$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{\\mathbf{v}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{v}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{v}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}+{x}_{2}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}+{x}_{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+20{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-13{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 20 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -13 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 5{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+2(\\mathbf{0})=\\mathbf{0}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(\\mathbf{0})+9(\\mathbf{0})=\\mathbf{0}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=\\mathbf{0}$, we get that the set $\\{{\\mathbf{a}}_{1},{\\mathbf{a}}_{2},{\\mathbf{a}}_{3}\\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are false, while statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186229709 :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle D(y) = \\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + y\\frac{dy}{dx}\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L$, we have the linearity condition\n$L(ay_{1} + by_{2}) = aL(y_{1}) + bL(y_{2})$ {{field-1}}\nfor functions $y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)$, and where $a$ and $b$ are constants." "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nUse this condition to determine whether $D$ is linear."] [:db/cas 17592186229709 :question/worked-solution nil "A linear differential operator $L$ satisfies\n$$\nL(ay_1+by_2)=aL(y_1)+bL(y_2)\n$$\nfor all suitable functions $y_1,y_2$ and constants $a,b.$ For the given operator $D(y)=y''+yy',$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(ay_1+by_2)&=ay_1''+by_2''+(ay_1+by_2)(ay_1'+by_2')\\\\\n&=ay_1''+by_2''+a^2y_1y_1'+b^2y_2y_2'+ab(y_1y_2'+y_2y_1').\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBy contrast,\n$$\naD(y_1)+bD(y_2)=ay_1''+by_2''+ay_1y_1'+by_2y_2'.\n$$\nThese expressions are not identical for all functions and constants. For example, $D(x)=x$ while $D(2x)=4x\\ne2D(x).$ Thus $D$ is nonlinear."] [:db/cas 17592186228429 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186228431] [:db/cas 17592186229749 :question/worked-solution nil "A linear differential operator is a sum of terms consisting of a function of $x$ times $y$ or one of its derivatives.\n\n- Operator I, $D_1(y)=xyy',$ is nonlinear because the coefficient $xy$ depends on $y.$ For example, $D_1(2x)=4x^2\\ne2D_1(x).$\n- Operator II, $D_2(y)=x^3y''-2x^2y',$ is linear: differentiation and multiplication by the fixed coefficient functions $x^3$ and $-2x^2$ preserve linear combinations.\n- Operator III, $D_3(y)=x^2y''-y,$ is linear for the same reason.\n\nTherefore the correct answer is **II and III only**."] [:db/cas 17592186228704 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186228706] [:db/cas 17592186265878 :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+a{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\left[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\left(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+2a{y}_{1}+2b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab\\left({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+2a{y}_{1}+2b{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a\\left[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+2a{y}_{1}+2b{y}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\left[\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+b{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\left[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\left(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+2a{y}_{1}+2b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab\\left({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\right)+2a{y}_{1}+2b{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a\\left[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+2a{y}_{1}+2b{y}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ax{y}_{1}+bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\left[\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186310953 :answer-field/correct 17592186309909 17592186309521] [:db/cas 17592186311402 :answer-field/correct 17592186310270 17592186309429] [:db/cas 17592186311094 :answer-field/correct 17592186309721 17592186309034] [:db/cas 17592186311440 :answer-field/correct 17592186309186 17592186308857] [:db/cas 17592186311224 :answer-field/correct 17592186309071 17592186310484] [:db/cas 17592186311381 :answer-field/correct 17592186310598 17592186309942] [:db/cas 17592186404044 :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\left[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-ax{y}_{1}-bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\left[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\left(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab\\left({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\na{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})=a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \n$$" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\left[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-x(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+bx\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-ax{y}_{1}-bx{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\left[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{1}\\right]+b\\left[x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x{y}_{2}\\right] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is **not** linear.\nTo see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\left(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab\\left({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\na{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})=a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228952 :question/problem "Show that the differential operator\n\n$$\nD(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nis nonlinear." "Show that the differential operator\n\n$$\nD(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x}\n$$\n\nis nonlinear."] [:db/cas 17592186229708 :question/worked-solution nil "A linear differential operator has coefficients that depend only on $x.$\n\n- Operator I, $D_1(y)=y'+2y,$ is linear, with constant coefficients.\n- Operator II, $D_2(y)=(\\sin x)y''+3x^2y'+2y,$ is linear, since its coefficients $\\sin x,$ $3x^2,$ and $2$ depend only on $x.$\n- Operator III, $D_3(y)=y'''+x\\cos(xy)y',$ is nonlinear because the coefficient $x\\cos(xy)$ depends on $y.$\n\nTherefore the correct answer is **I and II only**."] [:db/cas 17592186228423 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186228428] [:db/cas 17592186228933 :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y\n$$" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d} x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{2}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d} x}+3y\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{3}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{2}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186228933 :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator $D_1$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\cos(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator $D_2$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_2(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}, f_1(x) = -\\sin(x),$ and $f_0(x) = 3.$\n\n- Operator $D_3$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_3(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3(x) = 1, f_2(x) = e^{x}, f_1(x) = 0,$ and $f_0(x) = -\\ln(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{n}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{n-1}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d} x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator $D_1$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\cos(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator $D_2$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{2}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d} x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_2(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}, f_1(x) = -\\sin(x),$ and $f_0(x) = 3.$\n\n- Operator $D_3$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_3(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{3}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^{2}y}{\\textrm{d} x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d} x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3(x) = 1, f_2(x) = e^{x}, f_1(x) = 0,$ and $f_0(x) = -\\ln(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186229750 :question/worked-solution nil "Since $L$ is linear,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(y_1-2y_2)&=L(y_1)-2L(y_2)\\\\&=\\left(x+4-\\frac2x\\right)-2\\left(2-\\frac3x\\right)\\\\&=x+\\frac4x\\\\&=\\frac{x^2+4}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe identity holds wherever the given expressions are defined, so $x\\ne0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186228710 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186228714] [:db/cas 17592186230211 :tutorial/content "A differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following linearity condition is satisfied.\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_1(y) = x\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} +x^2y\n$$\n\nThis operator is indeed linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above holds. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince derivatives distribute over sums, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1})+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1})+bx\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{2})+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+b{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\underbrace{\\left(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1}\\right)}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{1})}+b\\underbrace{\\left(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2}\\right)}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $D_1(ay_1 + by_2) = aD_1(y_1) + bD_1(y_2),$ the operator $D_1$ is linear.\n\n- Now, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nThis operator is *not* linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above does not hold. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\left(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to $aD_2(y_1) + bD_2(y_2),$ we see that they are *not* the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\ne {D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the operator $D_2$ is *not* linear." "A differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following linearity condition is satisfied.\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_1(y) = x\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} +x^2y\n$$\n\nThis operator is indeed linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above holds. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) &=x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince derivatives distribute over sums, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1})+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1})+bx\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{2})+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+b{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a\\underbrace{\\left(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1}\\right)}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{1})}+b\\underbrace{\\left(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2}\\right)}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $D_1(ay_1 + by_2) = aD_1(y_1) + bD_1(y_2),$ the operator $D_1$ is linear.\n\n- Now, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nThis operator is *not* linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above does not hold. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\left(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\right) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to $aD_2(y_1) + bD_2(y_2),$ we see that they are *not* the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\ne {D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the operator $D_2$ is *not* linear."] [:db/cas 17592186152627 :question/worked-solution nil "A second-order ODE is linear when the coefficients of $y'',$ $y',$ and $y$ depend only on $x,$ and $y$ and its derivatives occur to the first power.\n\n- Equation I, $y''+2y'+5y=0,$ is linear, with constant coefficients.\n- Equation II, $3y''+y^2y'+4=0,$ is nonlinear because the coefficient $y^2$ of $y'$ depends on $y.$\n- Equation III, $y''-xy=x,$ is linear: its coefficients $1,$ $0,$ and $-x$ depend only on $x.$\n\nTherefore the correct answer is **I and III only**."] [:db/cas 17592186150269 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186150272] [:db/cas 17592186152631 :question/worked-solution nil "A second-order ODE is linear when it has the form $a(x)y''+b(x)y'+c(x)y=f(x),$ with coefficients depending only on $x$ and with $a(x)\\ne0$ on the interval considered.\n\n- Equation I, $xy''+2y'+(\\cos x)y=0,$ is linear on any interval avoiding $x=0.$\n- Equation II, $3y''+x^2y'+xy=4,$ is linear, since all its coefficients depend only on $x.$\n- Equation III, $y''-yy'=x,$ is nonlinear because the coefficient $-y$ of $y'$ depends on $y.$\n\nTherefore the correct answer is **I and II only**."] [:db/cas 17592186150293 :answer-field/correct nil 17592186150297] [:db/cas 17592186310010 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186309443 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186309437 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186309819 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186310760 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186309660 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186309634 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186309337 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186308752 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186310102 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186309791 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186308815 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186308860 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186310660 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186309054 :answer/type 17592186045498 17592186045499] [:db/cas 17592186266033 :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=\\pi $).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified." "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=\\pi$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."] [:db/ensure 17592186077756 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186077762 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186078116 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186078215 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186078335 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186078336 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186079163 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186079164 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186079223 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088057 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088058 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088287 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088288 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186088920 :tutorial/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186119497 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186119498 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186120147 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186120148 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186120149 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186120300 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186120318 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186120320 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186120321 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186124298 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186124299 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186124899 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186124907 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186124910 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186125158 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186150269 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186150293 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186152571 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186152627 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186152631 :question/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186228423 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186228429 :answer-field/validate] [:db/ensure 17592186228704 :answer-field/validat